     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "try:\n",
    "    df_policy = pd.read_excel(r\"C:\\Users\\vishwansingh\\OneDrive - Deloitte (O365D)\\Desktop\\Capstone_Project\\Insurance_Data\\Policy features.xlsx\")\n",
    "    df_car = pd.read_excel(r\"C:\\Users\\vishwansingh\\OneDrive - Deloitte (O365D)\\Desktop\\Capstone_Project\\Insurance_Data\\Car features.xlsx\")\n",
    "    df_claim = pd.read_excel(r\"C:\\Users\\vishwansingh\\OneDrive - Deloitte (O365D)\\Desktop\\Capstone_Project\\Insurance_Data\\Insurance claim.xlsx\")\n",
    "    print(\"Data loaded successfully \\n\")\n",
    "    print(f\"Policy Feature Shape:{df_policy.shape}\")\n",
    "    print(f\"Car Feature Shape:{df_car.shape}\")\n",
    "    print(f\"Insurance Claim Shape:{df_claim.shape}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "except Exception as e:\n",
    "    print(f\"Error loading data:{e}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "fb965b0e-8611-4af1-b24b-a4d2dae894b5",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Merging Strategy**\\\n",
    "Since the information is split across three files , we need to consolidate it into a single master Dataframe. The policy_id column acts as the unique identifier (primary key) across the file.\\\n",
    "we will use Outer Join for the merge. Because we want to identify any data anomalies such as policyholders who have car features but no claim record or vice-versa. \\\n",
    "An outer join ensures no records are dropped during the merge and also allowing us to explicitly investigate any missing mappings."
   ]
  },
