   "source": [
    "## Phase 5\n",
    "\n",
    "Univariate Analysis - Understanding Each Feature Individually \\\n",
    "In this phase, we look at the distribution of each variable independently. Our goal is to understand the underlying spread, central tendencies, and potential skewness in the data. Univariate analysis helps us spot anomalies, outliers, and dominant categories before we attempt to find relationships between variables."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "4ccf56b0-cefe-4677-a615-67dfeaffdea0",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**5.1 Target Variable Distribution**\n",
    "\n",
    "The target variable (`is_claim`) is what we are trying to predict. In insurance datasets, it is extremely common for the target to be highly imbalanced (i.e., very few claims compared to non-claims). Identifying this imbalance early is crucial because it will heavily influence our choice of evaluation metrics (e.g., F1-score) and modeling strategies (e.g., SMOTE, class weights)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 132,
   "id": "70b1ed72-4f92-4d3d-919e-488b42bd5536",
   "metadata": {
    "scrolled": true
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1400x600 with 2 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "import seaborn as sns\n",
    "COLORS = {\n",
    "    \"bg\":       \"#F5F5F5\",\n",
    "    \"no_claim\": \"#86BC25\",\n",
    "    \"claim\":    \"#DA291C\",\n",
    "    \"palette\":  [\"#DA291C\", \"#86BC25\"] \n",
    "}\n",
    "fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(14, 6))\n",
    "fig.patch.set_facecolor(COLORS['bg'])\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "ax1 = sns.countplot(\n",
    "    x='is_claim',\n",
    "    data=df_clean,\n",
    "    palette=COLORS['palette'],         \n",
    "    ax=axes[0]\n",
    ")\n",
    "axes[0].set_title('Count of No Claims vs Claims', fontsize=14, pad=15)\n",
    "axes[0].set_xlabel('Is Claim (0 = No, 1 = Yes)', fontsize=12)\n",
    "axes[0].set_ylabel('Count', fontsize=12)\n",
    "\n",
    "for p in ax1.patches:\n",
    "    ax1.annotate(\n",
    "        format(p.get_height(), '.0f'),\n",
    "        (p.get_x() + p.get_width() / 2., p.get_height()),\n",
    "        ha='center', va='center',\n",
    "        xytext=(0, 9),\n",
    "        textcoords='offset points',\n",
    "        fontsize=12, fontweight='bold'\n",
    "    )\n",
    "\n",
    "claim_counts = df_clean['is_claim'].value_counts()\n",
    "axes[1].pie(\n",
    "    claim_counts,\n",
    "    labels=['No Claim (0)', 'Claim (1)'],\n",
    "    autopct='%1.1f%%',\n",
    "    colors=COLORS['palette'],           \n",
    "    startangle=90,\n",
    "    explode=[0, 0.1],\n",
    "    textprops={'fontsize': 12, 'fontweight': 'bold'},\n",
    "    shadow=True\n",
    ")\n",
    "axes[1].set_title('Proportion of Claims', fontsize=14, pad=15)\n",
    "plt.suptitle(\n",
    "    'Target Variable (is_claim) Distribution',\n",
    "    fontsize=18, fontweight='bold', y=1.05\n",
    ")\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c2fefc39-15cd-4132-86d5-3261d96780bd",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Key Findings**\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Severe Class Imbalance\n",
    "The target variable is_claim is highly imbalanced, with a ≈14.6:1 ratio between the majority class (No Claim) and the minority class (Claim). For every 1 policyholder who files a claim, nearly 15 did not — a level of imbalance considered severe in predictive modeling.\n",
    "2. Dominant Majority Class\n",
    "93.6% of policyholders did not file a claim, making \"No Claim\" the overwhelming majority class. This is a realistic reflection of insurance data, where claims are inherently rare events.\n",
    "3. Small but Critical Minority Class\n",
    "The Claim class (1) accounts for only 6.4% (3,743 records) of the data. Despite being the minority, this is precisely the class the model must predict accurately. It is the business-critical outcome."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c7971395-2bf0-444e-ba1f-8dcbf46c2011",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**5.2 Numerical Features Distribution**\n",
    "\n",
    "We will visualize the distributions of our numerical features using histograms overlaid with Kernel Density Estimation (KDE) curves. This helps us see if variables follow a normal distribution or if they are skewed. We will also plot the mean and median to better understand the central tendency of each feature."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 133,
   "id": "9fcf3f9f-2b7b-40d9-94a7-bbdf71c1dbe5",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1800x2800 with 19 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "import math\n",
    "n_cols = 3\n",
    "n_rows = math.ceil(len(num_cols) / n_cols)\n",
    "fig, axes = plt.subplots(n_rows, n_cols, figsize=(18, n_rows * 4))\n",
    "fig.patch.set_facecolor(COLORS['bg'])\n",
    "axes = axes.flatten()\n",
    "for i, col in enumerate(num_cols):\n",
    "    ax = axes[i]\n",
    "    data = df_clean[col].dropna()\n",
    "    sns.histplot(data, kde=True, color='#FFD700', ax=ax, bins=30)\n",
    "    mean_val = data.mean()\n",
    "    median_val = data.median()\n",
    "    ax.axvline(mean_val, color='r', linestyle='--', linewidth=2, label=f'Mean: {mean_val:.2f}')\n",
    "    ax.axvline(median_val, color='g', linestyle='-', linewidth=2, label=f'Median: {median_val:.2f}')\n",
    "    ax.set_title(f'Distribution of {col}', fontsize=12, fontweight='bold')\n",
    "    ax.set_xlabel('')\n",
    "    ax.set_ylabel('Frequency')\n",
    "    ax.legend(fontsize=9)\n",
    "for j in range(i + 1, len(axes)):\n",
    "    fig.delaxes(axes[j])\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "64261a93-86bf-4c8c-8a8d-7620d213fb37",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Observations: Distribution of Numerical Features**\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "    . policy_tenure (Mean: 0.61, Median: 0.57): Bimodal distribution with peaks near 0.05 and 1.0, mildly right-skewed, indicating two groups — new and near-mature policyholders.\n",
    "    . age_of_car (Mean: 0.07, Median: 0.06): Heavily right-skewed with most cars being very new. A long tail indicates few older vehicles. Log transformation recommended.\n",
    "    . age_of_policyholder (Mean: 0.47, Median: 0.45): Right-skewed with peak around 0.30–0.35, suggesting younger policyholders dominate, with fewer older ones forming the right tail.\n",
    "    . population_density (Mean: 18,828, Median: 8,794): Most severely right-skewed feature. Mean is more than double the median, driven by extreme high-density outliers. Log transformation strongly recommended.\n",
    "    . make (Mean: 1.76, Median: 1.00): Heavily right-skewed with most records at make = 1. Behaves as a quasi-categorical variable.\n",
    "    . airbags (Mean: 3.14, Median: 2.00): Right-skewed discrete distribution. Most vehicles have 2 airbags, with a secondary spike at 6, inflating the mean.\n",
    "    . displacement (Mean: 1162.29, Median: 1197.00): Clearly bimodal with two peaks at ~800cc and ~1500cc, representing small and mid-range engine segments. Mildly left-skewed.\n",
    "    . cylinder (Mean: 3.63, Median: 4.00): Bimodal discrete distribution with peaks at 3 and 4 cylinders. Mildly left-skewed; behaves as a categorical feature.\n",
    "    . gear_box (Mean: 5.25, Median: 5.00): Bimodal discrete distribution dominated by 5-gear vehicles, with a secondary spike at 6 gears. Mildly right-skewed.\n",
    "    . turning_radius (Mean: 4.85, Median: 4.80): Multimodal with peaks around 4.6, 4.7, and 4.8m. Mean and median closely aligned — near-symmetric overall.\n",
    "    . length (Mean: 3850.38, Median: 3845.00): Multimodal with peaks at ~3800mm and ~4000mm. Near-symmetric with mean ≈ median, reflecting different car body classes.\n",
    "    . width (Mean: 1672.21, Median: 1735.00): Multimodal with peaks at ~1500mm, ~1750mm, and ~1800mm. Left-skewed, with narrower vehicles pulling the mean down.\n",
    "    . height (Mean: 1553.32, Median: 1530.00): Bimodal with peaks at ~1500mm and ~1640mm, representing sedans/hatchbacks and taller SUVs. Mildly right-skewed.\n",
    "    . gross_weight (Mean: 1385.23, Median: 1335.00): Multimodal with peaks at ~1200, ~1335, and ~1500 kg. Mildly right-skewed, with heavier vehicles pulling the mean up.\n",
    "    . ncap_rating (Mean: 1.76, Median: 2.00): Bimodal with a large spike at 0 (unrated vehicles) and another at 3. Mildly left-skewed.\n",
    "    . torque_nm (Mean: 134.43, Median: 113.00): Right-skewed with multiple peaks. High-torque outliers significantly inflate the mean. Outlier treatment and log transformation recommended.\n",
    "    . torque_rpm (Mean: 3533.24, Median: 3500.00): Multimodal with peaks at ~2500, ~3000, ~3500, and ~4500 RPM. Near-symmetric with mean ≈ median.\n",
    "    . power_bhp (Mean: 78.97, Median: 88.50): Bimodal with clusters at ~45 BHP and ~88–90 BHP. Left-skewed, with lower-power vehicles pulling the mean below the median.\n",
    "    . power_rpm (Mean: 5307.39, Median: 6000.00): Strongly bimodal with two sharp peaks at 4000 RPM and 6000 RPM (dominant). Left-skewed, likely representing diesel vs. petrol engine types."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "0064bf6c-bf54-4577-b1c1-be86fbaf5fe0",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**5.3 Categorical Features Distribution**\n",
    "Now we will look at the frequency of different categories within our categorical features to identify the most and least common groups. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 134,
   "id": "68a572d3-b225-4d30-af84-7cfe070c96fe",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 2000x2400 with 8 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "import math\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "import matplotlib.patches as mpatches\n",
    "import seaborn as sns\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "ATTRACTIVE_PALETTE = [\n",
    "    \"#6C63FF\", \"#FF6584\", \"#43C6AC\", \"#F7971E\",\n",
    "    \"#00C9FF\", \"#F953C6\", \"#B2FF59\", \"#FF6B6B\",\n",
    "    \"#48C774\", \"#FFD93D\"\n",
    "]\n",
    "BG_COLOR      = \"#1E1E2F\"   \n",
    "AXES_COLOR    = \"#2A2A40\"   \n",
    "TITLE_COLOR   = \"#FFFFFF\"\n",
    "LABEL_COLOR   = \"#CFCFDF\"\n",
    "ANNOT_COLOR   = \"#FFFFFF\"\n",
    "GRID_COLOR    = \"#3A3A55\"\n",
    "SPINE_COLOR   = \"#44445A\"\n",
    "n_cols_cat  = 2\n",
    "n_rows_cat  = math.ceil(len(cat_cols) / n_cols_cat)\n",
    "fig, axes = plt.subplots(\n",
    "    n_rows_cat, n_cols_cat,\n",
    "    figsize=(20, n_rows_cat * 6)\n",
    ")\n",
    "fig.patch.set_facecolor(BG_COLOR)\n",
    "fig.suptitle(\n",
    "    \"Categorical Feature Distributions\",\n",
    "    fontsize=22, fontweight=\"bold\",\n",
    "    color=TITLE_COLOR, y=1.01\n",
    ")\n",
    "axes = axes.flatten() if n_rows_cat > 1 else [axes] if n_cols_cat == 1 else axes.flatten()\n",
    "for i, col in enumerate(cat_cols):\n",
    "    ax  = axes[i]\n",
    "    counts = df_clean[col].value_counts()\n",
    "    n_bars = len(counts)\n",
    "    bar_colors = [ATTRACTIVE_PALETTE[k % len(ATTRACTIVE_PALETTE)] for k in range(n_bars)]\n",
    "    bars = ax.bar(\n",
    "        range(n_bars),\n",
    "        counts.values,\n",
    "        color=bar_colors,\n",
    "        width=0.55,\n",
    "        edgecolor=\"white\",\n",
    "        linewidth=0.6,\n",
    "        zorder=3\n",
    "    )\n",
    "    ax.set_facecolor(AXES_COLOR)\n",
    "    ax.set_title(f\"Distribution of  {col}\", fontsize=14, fontweight=\"bold\",\n",
    "                 color=TITLE_COLOR, pad=12)\n",
    "    ax.set_xlabel(col, fontsize=11, color=LABEL_COLOR, labelpad=8)\n",
    "    ax.set_ylabel(\"Count\", fontsize=11, color=LABEL_COLOR, labelpad=8)\n",
    "    ax.set_xticks(range(n_bars))\n",
    "    ax.set_xticklabels(\n",
    "        counts.index,\n",
    "        rotation=45 if n_bars > 5 else 0,\n",
    "        ha=\"right\" if n_bars > 5 else \"center\",\n",
    "        fontsize=10, color=LABEL_COLOR\n",
    "    )\n",
    "    ax.tick_params(axis=\"y\", colors=LABEL_COLOR)\n",
    "    for spine in ax.spines.values():\n",
    "        spine.set_edgecolor(SPINE_COLOR)\n",
    "    ax.yaxis.grid(True, color=GRID_COLOR, linestyle=\"--\", linewidth=0.6, zorder=0)\n",
    "    ax.set_axisbelow(True)\n",
    "    y_max = counts.values.max()\n",
    "    ax.set_ylim(0, y_max * 1.18)\n",
    "    for bar, val in zip(bars, counts.values):\n",
    "        ax.annotate(\n",
    "            f\"{val:,}\",\n",
    "            xy=(bar.get_x() + bar.get_width() / 2, bar.get_height()),\n",
    "            xytext=(0, 6),\n",
    "            textcoords=\"offset points\",\n",
    "            ha=\"center\", va=\"bottom\",\n",
    "            fontsize=10, fontweight=\"bold\",\n",
    "            color=ANNOT_COLOR\n",
    "        )\n",
    "for j in range(i + 1, len(axes)):\n",
    "    fig.delaxes(axes[j])\n",
    "plt.tight_layout(pad=3.0)\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "34023249-2e15-49c8-be02-b6d5d2e5cdc5",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Observations**\n",
    "\n",
    "    . area_cluster : C8 is the dominant cluster with 13,630 records, followed by C2 (7,335) and C5 (6,969) — indicating a heavy geographic concentration in a few areas. The distribution has a long tail: clusters like C20 (109), C22 (207), and C18 (240) are extremely rare .\n",
    "    . segment : B2 (18,295) and A (17,300) are the dominant vehicle segments, together accounting for nearly 60% of the dataset. Utility (1,208) is the least represented segment and may need special treatment or oversampling if it is a target-related feature.\n",
    "    . model : The top 3 models — M1 (14,934), M4 (13,991), and M6 (13,763) — are nearly equally distributed, suggesting the dataset is reasonably balanced for these. M11 (363) is severely underrepresented and risks being unreliable for model learning without grouping or augmentation.\n",
    "    . fuel_type : All three fuel types are well-balanced: Petrol (20,495), CNG (20,300), and Diesel (17,694) — no significant imbalance. This feature is unlikely to introduce class bias.\n",
    "    . engine_type : The distribution mirrors model closely, with the top 3 engines (F8D Petrol Engine, 1.5L U2 CRDi, K Series Dual Jet) dominating, suggesting a high correlation between model and engine_type.The rarest engine is (1.5 Turbocharged Revotron, 363).\n",
    "    . rear_brakes_type : Heavily imbalanced: Drum brakes (44,524) account for ~76% of records vs. Disc (13,991). Models trained on this feature should account for this imbalance, especially if brakes type is predictive of the target.\n",
    "    . transmission_type : Manual (38,145) dominates with ~65% share vs. Automatic (20,370). While not severely imbalanced, this skew should be noted when evaluating model predictions segmented by transmission.\n",
    "    . steering_type : Power steering (33,456) is the most common, followed by Electric (23,851). Manual steering (1,208) is nearly negligible (~2%) and is a candidate for merging with another category or flagging as a rare class."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e246a93d-fc02-4fde-8ec5-f770a2a0120a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**5.4 Boolean Features Distribution**\n",
    "\n",
    "We have a large number of boolean features, primarily representing various car safety and convenience features. A stacked bar chart will give us a quick bird's-eye view of how prevalent each feature is across the dataset."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 135,
   "id": "f8885c54-4235-469a-a8a6-5a384785470f",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1200x1000 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "bool_proportions = []\n",
    "for col in bool_cols:\n",
    "    counts = df_clean[col].value_counts(normalize=True).reindex([0, 1], fill_value=0) * 100\n",
    "    bool_proportions.append({'Feature': col, 'No (0)': counts[0], 'Yes (1)': counts[1]})\n",
    "df_bool_prop = pd.DataFrame(bool_proportions).set_index('Feature')\n",
    "df_bool_prop = df_bool_prop.sort_values(by='Yes (1)')\n",
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(12, 10))\n",
    "fig.patch.set_facecolor(COLORS['bg'])\n",
    "df_bool_prop.plot(kind='barh', stacked=True, color=['#e74c3c', '#2ecc71'], ax=ax)\n",
    "ax.set_title('Prevalence of Boolean (Safety/Convenience) Features', fontsize=16, fontweight='bold')\n",
    "ax.set_xlabel('Percentage (%)', fontsize=12)\n",
    "ax.set_ylabel('Feature', fontsize=12)\n",
    "ax.legend(title='Present?', loc='upper right', bbox_to_anchor=(1.15, 1))\n",
    "ax.set_xlim(0, 100)\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e2a02540-b5d4-4486-9a5f-38b5853ca66a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Observations**\n",
    "\n",
    "    . Universally Present Features (>95% Yes) : is_speed_alert (~100%) and is_power_steering (~97%) are present in virtually every vehicle in the dataset and these features carry almost zero discriminative power for modeling and can be safely dropped or deprioritized.\n",
    "    . Near-Universal Features (~95%) : is_parking_sensors (~95% Yes) is also near-universal, suggesting it has become a standard fitment across most variants.\n",
    "    . Moderately Present Features (~70–75% Yes) : is_central_locking, is_power_door_locks, and is_ecw all hover around 72% prevalence and these are common but not universal, making them moderately informative for the model.\n",
    "    . Roughly Balanced Features (~55–60% Yes) : is_adjustable_steering, is_driver_seat_height_adjustable, and is_front_fog_lights sit close to the 50–60% range and these are the most informative binary features as they provide the most entropy/split value for tree-based models.\n",
    "    . Minority Features (<40% Yes) : is_parking_camera (~40%), is_day_night_rear_view_mirror (~38%), is_rear_window_defogger (~35%), is_esc (~32%), is_rear_window_wiper and is_rear_window_washer (~30%) are premium/optional features and absent in the majority of vehicles, likely correlated with higher-trim variants.\n",
    "    . Rarest Feature : is_tpms (~25% Yes) is the least prevalent safety feature, present in only 1 in 4 vehicles are likely restricted to top-end trims. It could serve as a strong proxy for vehicle segment/trim level."
   ]
  },
