    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e3dbc25a-cfa8-41b4-a2a9-57208e067e9f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Inference**\n",
    "\n",
    "    . The boxplot is essentially uninformative here. Both claim (1) and no-claim (0) groups show an identical distribution: median ≈ 6, IQR from 2 to 6, whisker to 1. Airbags is a discrete count (values 1, 2, 6), so a boxplot compresses it into near-identical boxes and hides the real signal. The right-hand rate plot is the one that matters.\n",
    "    . Claim rate rises slightly with more airbags, but the effect is tiny. The range is only ~6.05% (1 airbag) → ~6.37% (2) → ~6.49% (6). That's a spread of about 0.44 percentage points across the whole range. Vehicles with 2+ airbags sit at or just above the overall mean; single-airbag vehicles sit just below.\n",
    "    . Counterintuitive direction, weak magnitude. More airbags is a safety feature, so you might expect lower claim rates. The mild upward trend more likely reflects a confounder and more airbags tend to appear on newer/pricier/larger vehicles, which may have different claim behavior and rather than airbags causing claims."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "f95cce2d-2cfd-4bee-b1a2-8884dc39a674",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
