   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "791d0e9e-4ae8-4427-8316-1e7dfa4cdd24",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Phase 8"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "0eaa188a-ee27-47fb-850d-098c7dbab645",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Multivariate Analysis & Feature Importance**\n",
    "\n",
    "In this section, we transition from examining variables in isolation (or pairs) to understanding how they interact with each other and crucially which features are the strongest drivers of our target variable (is_claim). Feature importance helps us prioritize variables for modeling and identify potential multicollinearity (when features are highly correlated with each other)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "1376d695-b622-4617-b1f5-5d076f1cc739",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**8.1 Correlation Matrix**\n",
    "\n",
    "A correlation matrix shows the linear relationship between all numerical variables. Why it matters:\n",
    "\n",
    "    . Feature-to-Target Relationship: We can see which numerical features naturally move with our target.\n",
    "    . Multicollinearity: If two features are highly correlated (e.g., displacement and power_bhp), they provide redundant information. This can destabilize some machine learning models (like Linear/Logistic Regression)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 155,
   "id": "6b3130c3-e642-4913-b2ce-adb0d757e5bf",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
