   "source": [
    "corr_cols = num_cols + bool_cols + ['is_claim']\n",
    "df_corr = df_clean[corr_cols].dropna()\n",
    "\n",
    "corr_matrix = df_corr.corr()\n",
    "\n",
    "mask = np.triu(np.ones_like(corr_matrix, dtype=bool))\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(24, 20))\n",
    "sns.heatmap(corr_matrix, mask=mask, cmap='RdBu_r', annot=False, fmt=\".2f\", \n",
    "            linewidths=0.5, vmin=-1, vmax=1, center=0, cbar_kws={\"shrink\": .8})\n",
    "plt.title(\"Correlation Matrix of Numerical and Boolean Features\", fontsize=20, pad=20)\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c161f24a-e726-4699-a222-3bf4f18030ad",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Key Observations:**\n",
    "\n",
    "    . Target (is_claim): The bottom row is almost entirely pale/neutral — is_claim has near-zero linear correlation with every feature. No single numeric or boolean variable is a strong linear predictor of claims.\n",
    "    . Strong feature clusters (multicollinearity): A large deep-red block links the vehicle spec/feature variables — displacement, cylinder, gear_box, turning_radius, length, width, gross_weight, torque_nm, power_bhp, and the comfort/safety booleans (is_tpms, is_esc, is_rear_window_, is_brake_assist, is_power_door_locks, is_central_locking, is_ecw). These move together — bigger/higher-spec cars carry more features.\n",
    "    . Notable negatives: power_rpm and torque_rpm are strongly negatively correlated (deep blue) with most spec features — higher-displacement engines make peak power/torque at lower RPM, as expected mechanically.\n",
    "    . Redundancy pairs (~0.9+): e.g. is_rear_window_washer, is_rear_window_wiper, is_power_door_locks↔is_central_locking, is_ecw, is_central_locking. \n",
    "    . Key takeaway: No feature correlates meaningfully with is_claim → linear models will struggle; expect weak signal and consider non-linear models (tree-based) plus engineered/interaction features. Meanwhile, heavy multicollinearity among vehicle-spec features means you should drop or combine redundant columns (or use PCA/regularization) before modeling."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "758dc428-b6c5-42da-b915-b4132a0dab16",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**8.2 Correlation with Target**\n",
    "\n",
    "This is the most direct measure of simple linear feature importance. We isolate the correlation of every feature specifically with our target variable is_claim to rank them."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 156,
   "id": "8472e3e8-c0cb-4253-8988-d77bc3f2d7b5",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1200x1400 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "target_corr = corr_matrix['is_claim'].drop('is_claim')\n",
    "\n",
    "target_corr_sorted = target_corr.reindex(target_corr.abs().sort_values(ascending=False).index)\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(12, 14))\n",
    "colors = ['#2ecc71' if x > 0 else '#e74c3c' for x in target_corr_sorted.values]\n",
    "bars = plt.barh(target_corr_sorted.index[::-1], target_corr_sorted.values[::-1], color=colors[::-1])\n",
    "\n",
    "for bar in bars:\n",
    "    width = bar.get_width()\n",
    "    label_x_pos = width + 0.001 if width > 0 else width - 0.001\n",
    "    ha = 'left' if width > 0 else 'right'\n",
    "    plt.text(label_x_pos, bar.get_y() + bar.get_height()/2, f'{width:.4f}', \n",
    "             va='center', ha=ha, fontsize=9)\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.title(\"Feature Correlation with Target (is_claim)\", fontsize=16)\n",
    "plt.xlabel(\"Pearson Correlation Coefficient\")\n",
    "plt.ylabel(\"Feature\")\n",
    "plt.axvline(0, color='black', linewidth=1, linestyle='--')\n",
    "from matplotlib.patches import Patch\n",
    "legend_elements = [Patch(facecolor='#2ecc71', label='Positive Correlation'),\n",
    "                   Patch(facecolor='#e74c3c', label='Negative Correlation')]\n",
    "plt.legend(handles=legend_elements, loc='lower right')\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "5649f98c-d382-459b-ab6e-cf524faeb2c6",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Key Observations:**\n",
    "\n",
    "All correlations are extremely weak. The strongest feature, policy_tenure, reaches only +0.079 , everything else is below |0.03|. No feature has a meaningful linear relationship with is_claim.\n",
    "\n",
    "Top positive signals (still tiny):\n",
    "\n",
    "    . policy_tenure (+0.0791) means longer tenure slightly associated with more claims\n",
    "    . age_of_policyholder (+0.0224)\n",
    "    . is_adjustable_steering (+0.0140), cylinder (+0.0136)\n",
    "\n",
    "Top negative signals (tiny):\n",
    "\n",
    "    . age_of_car (−0.0279) means newer/older cars marginally fewer claims\n",
    "    . population_density (−0.0179)\n",
    "\n",
    "Vehicle-spec & comfort features are near-zero (length, torque, gross_weight, is_esc, is_tpms, etc. all < 0.007) which confirming they carry essentially no standalone linear signal for claims.\n",
    "\n",
    "Key takeaway: Pearson correlation shows no strong linear predictor of is_claim. This does not mean the features are useless infact it means:\n",
    "\n",
    "    . Linear models (logistic regression) will underperform without feature engineering.\n",
    "    . Signal is likely non-linear or interaction-based → favor tree-based models (RandomForest, XGBoost, LightGBM) that capture these automatically.\n",
    "    . Confirm importance later with model-based feature importance / SHAP, not just correlation, since Pearson misses non-linear and categorical effects.\n",
    "    . Combined with the earlier heavy multicollinearity, prioritize policy_tenure, age_of_car, age_of_policyholder, and population_density as the most promising raw numeric features to build on."
   ]
  }
